  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 1230 AM MDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nTuesday...Upper ridge starts to flatten and migrate west back across \nthe Desert SW, shifting the flow aloft to a more westerly direction. \nThis opens the door for an initial weak cold front, from the first \nupper low center moving across MT, to drop south and reach the \nforecast area Tue morning. While this front should cool max temps by \nabout 3-4 degrees across the plains, favorable llvl wind flow will \nhelp spark more convection across the higher terrain and plains, \nwith a focus on the eastern mts and I-25 Corridor by late afternoon \ninto the early evening. The Aspen Acres and 24 burn scars will need \nto be monitored closely for potential stronger storm development \nsince storm movement will likely be somewhat slower. Plan on highs \nin the 80s for the high valleys, and mid 80s to mid 90s for the \nplains.\n\nWednesday...Flat upper ridge remains across the southern US, with \nthe high center building back across northern AZ. At the same time, \nthe second low center to the north pushing along the MT and Canada \nborder late Tue night will drop a stronger cold front south into \neastern CO. This will aid in even more cooling across the eastern \nplains, as well as another round of enhanced convective activity \nthough it may be slightly delayed. Initially, showers and storms \nwill likely develop along the southern border and in the vicinity of \nthe southern Sangres, Trinidad and Walsenburg in the afternoon. As \nthe surface winds then shift to a more east to southeast direction, \nthen activity will develop northward across all of the plains and \nalong the I-25 Corridor. This will be another day of watching area \nburn scars for potential flooding. Expect high temps in the 80s for \nmost locations.\n\nThursday and Friday...Broad upper ridge will remain in place across \nthe Desert SW to end the work week, providing for essentially zonal \nflow aloft, a gradual warming trend and very dry conditions, with \njust isolated convection forecast both days.\n\nSaturday and Sunday...Long range models seem to agree on shifting \nthe upper high to the east in the southeastern US over the weekend, \nallowing another strong low pressure system to drop down out of the \nPacific NW. This will bring a cooling trend with better chances for \nwidespread moisture, starting on Saturday but moreso for Sunday. \nMoore",
